  "body": "### **Vulnerability details**\n\n#### Finding description and impact\nThe `Distributor._claim` function contains a critical flaw in its handling of callbacks to recipient contracts. When a user claims rewards using `claimWithRecipient` and specifies a contract recipient with callback data, the `Distributor` attempts to call `onClaim` on the recipient. This external call is wrapped in a `try/catch {}` block that completely swallows any error, including reverts.\n\n```solidity\n// contracts/Distributor.sol#L481-L485\ntry IClaimRecipient(recipient).onClaim(user, token, amount, data) returns (bytes32 callbackSuccess) {\n    if (callbackSuccess != CALLBACK_SUCCESS) revert Errors.InvalidReturnMessage();\n} catch {}\n```\n\nThis design creates a dangerous silent failure mode. If the recipient contract's `onClaim` function reverts for any reason (e.g., a downstream protocol is paused, slippage is exceeded, or a parameter is invalid), the `catch` block prevents the main `claim` transaction from reverting. The `Distributor` contract proceeds as if the operation was successful: it updates the user's `claimed` amount and considers the reward fully paid.\n\nThis breaks the atomicity of the claim-and-act operation. The reward tokens are successfully transferred to the recipient contract, but the intended subsequent action (e.g., staking, swapping) fails. The user's rewards become stranded in the recipient contract.\n\n**Impact:**\n\nThis vulnerability leads to the **permanent loss of user funds** under realistic conditions. A user might employ a trusted \"claim-and-stake\" contract as their recipient. If the underlying staking protocol is temporarily unavailable when the claim is executed, the funds will be transferred, the callback will fail silently, and the `Distributor` will mark the rewards as fully claimed.\n\nThe user cannot re-attempt the claim, as their balance has been updated. If the recipient contract was not designed with a specific recovery function for such stranded funds, the user's rewards are permanently locked and irrecoverable. This directly violates the sponsor's primary security concern of **\"Reward Claim Integrity\"** and constitutes an irreversible loss of user assets.\n\n#### Recommended mitigation steps\nThe silent `catch {}` block should be removed entirely. A failure in a recipient's callback is a critical event that indicates the user's intent was not fully executed. The entire `claim` transaction must revert to ensure atomicity. This allows the user to safely retry the transaction later when the external conditions are favorable (e.g., the downstream protocol is unpaused).\n\nApply the following diff to `contracts/Distributor.sol`:\n\n```diff\n--- a/contracts/Distributor.sol\n+++ b/contracts/Distributor.sol\n@@ -480,9 +480,8 @@\n             if (toSend != 0) {\n                 IERC20(token).safeTransfer(recipient, toSend);\n                 if (data.length != 0) {\n-                    try IClaimRecipient(recipient).onClaim(user, token, amount, data) returns (bytes32 callbackSuccess) {\n-                        if (callbackSuccess != CALLBACK_SUCCESS) revert Errors.InvalidReturnMessage();\n-                    } catch {}\n+                    bytes32 callbackSuccess = IClaimRecipient(recipient).onClaim(user, token, amount, data);\n+                    if (callbackSuccess != CALLBACK_SUCCESS) revert Errors.InvalidReturnMessage();\n                 }\n             }\n             unchecked {\n\n```",
  "proof_of_concept": "### **Proof of Concept (PoC)**\n\nThe following test case demonstrates that a reverting recipient contract causes funds to be permanently locked. The test passes, proving that the `claimWithRecipient` call succeeds on-chain even though the recipient's callback reverts, trapping the funds.\n\n#### Instructions:\n1.  Replace the contents of `test/c4/C4PoC.t.sol` with the code below.\n2.  Run the test with the command: `forge test --match-test submissionValidity -vv`.\n\n#### PoC Code:\n```solidity\n// test/c4/C4PoC.t.sol\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: UNLICENSED\npragma solidity ^0.8.17;\n\nimport { Fixture } from \"../Fixture.t.sol\";\nimport { Distributor, MerkleTree } from \"../../contracts/Distributor.sol\";\nimport { IClaimRecipient } from \"../../contracts/interfaces/IClaimRecipient.sol\";\nimport { console } from \"forge-std/console.sol\";\nimport { Errors } from \"../../contracts/utils/Errors.sol\";\nimport { IAccessControlManager } from \"../../contracts/interfaces/IAccessControlManager.sol\";\n\n// A mock recipient contract that always reverts on `onClaim`.\n// It has no other functions, simulating a contract where funds can be trapped.\ncontract RevertingRecipient is IClaimRecipient {\n    bytes32 public constant CALLBACK_SUCCESS = keccak256(\"IClaimRecipient.onClaim\");\n    function onClaim(address, address, uint256, bytes calldata) external pure returns (bytes32) {\n        revert(\"Downstream protocol is paused!\");\n    }\n}\n\ncontract C4PoC is Fixture {\n    Distributor public distributor;\n    RevertingRecipient public revertingContract;\n\n    function setUp() public override {\n        super.setUp();\n\n        // Deploy and set up a new Distributor instance\n        Distributor distributorImpl = new Distributor();\n        bytes memory initData = abi.encodeCall(\n            Distributor.initialize,\n            (IAccessControlManager(address(accessControlManager)))\n        );\n        distributor = Distributor(payable(deployUUPS(address(distributorImpl), initData)));\n\n        vm.prank(governor);\n        distributor.setDisputePeriod(0); // Disable dispute period for simplicity\n\n        // Deploy the reverting recipient contract\n        revertingContract = new RevertingRecipient();\n    }\n\n    function test_submissionValidity() public {\n        // ================== PoC Setup ==================\n        address user = alice;\n        uint256 rewardAmount = 100 ether;\n        angle.mint(address(distributor), rewardAmount);\n\n        // 1. Create a valid Merkle tree where Alice can claim rewards.\n        bytes32 leaf = keccak256(abi.encode(user, address(angle), rewardAmount));\n        bytes32[] memory proof; // Proof for a single-leaf tree\n        bytes32 root = leaf;\n        vm.prank(governor);\n        distributor.updateTree(MerkleTree(root, bytes32(0)));\n        vm.warp(distributor.endOfDisputePeriod() + 1);\n\n        console.log(\"--- Initial State ---\");\n        console.log(\"Alice's ANGLE balance: %s\", angle.balanceOf(user));\n        console.log(\"Reverting contract's ANGLE balance: %s\", angle.balanceOf(address(revertingContract)));\n\n        // ================== Exploit Execution ==================\n        console.log(\"\\n--- The Attack ---\");\n        console.log(\"Alice calls `claimWithRecipient`, targeting the reverting contract...\");\n\n        // 2. Alice calls `claimWithRecipient`. The call will succeed due to the silent try/catch.\n        vm.startPrank(user);\n        address[] memory users = new address[](1); users[0] = user;\n        address[] memory tokens = new address[](1); tokens[0] = address(angle);\n        uint256[] memory amounts = new uint256[](1); amounts[0] = rewardAmount;\n        bytes32[][] memory proofs = new bytes32[][](1); proofs[0] = proof;\n        address[] memory recipients = new address[](1); recipients[0] = address(revertingContract);\n        bytes[] memory datas = new bytes[](1); datas[0] = abi.encode(\"stake()\");\n        distributor.claimWithRecipient(users, tokens, amounts, proofs, recipients, datas);\n        vm.stopPrank();\n\n        // ================== Verification ==================\n        console.log(\"\\n--- After Silent Failure ---\");\n\n        // ASSERT 1: Funds are TRAPPED in the recipient contract.\n        assertEq(angle.balanceOf(user), 0, \"Alice should not have received the funds directly.\");\n        assertEq(angle.balanceOf(address(revertingContract)), rewardAmount, \"Funds should be TRAPPED in the reverting contract.\");\n        console.log(\"Alice's ANGLE balance: %s (Unchanged)\", angle.balanceOf(user));\n        console.log(\"Reverting contract's ANGLE balance: %s (Funds are trapped!)\", angle.balanceOf(address(revertingContract)));\n\n        // ASSERT 2: The Distributor has marked the claim as completed.\n        (uint208 claimedAmount,,) = distributor.claimed(user, address(angle));\n        assertEq(claimedAmount, rewardAmount, \"Distributor should have marked the rewards as claimed.\");\n        console.log(\"Distributor recorded Alice's claim as complete.\");\n\n        // ASSERT 3: Alice cannot claim again.\n        console.log(\"\\nAlice tries to claim again...\");\n        vm.startPrank(user);\n        distributor.claimWithRecipient(users, tokens, amounts, proofs, recipients, datas);\n        vm.stopPrank();\n        assertEq(angle.balanceOf(address(revertingContract)), rewardAmount, \"Trapped funds remain trapped, no double payment.\");\n        console.log(\"Second claim attempt does nothing as expected.\");\n\n        console.log(\"\\n[SUCCESS] PoC successful. The claim succeeded on-chain, but the funds are permanently locked in the recipient contract due to the silent failure.\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\n#### Test Output:\n```bash\n$ forge test --match-test submissionValidity -vv\n[PASS] test_submissionValidity() (gas: 267381)\nLogs:\n  --- Initial State ---\n  Alice's ANGLE balance: 0\n  Reverting contract's ANGLE balance: 0\n\n--- The Attack ---\n  Alice calls `claimWithRecipient`, targeting the reverting contract...\n\n--- After Silent Failure ---\n  Alice's ANGLE balance: 0 (Unchanged)\n  Reverting contract's ANGLE balance: 100000000000000000000 (Funds are trapped!)\n  Distributor recorded Alice's claim as complete.\n\nAlice tries to claim again...\n  Second claim attempt does nothing as expected.\n\n[SUCCESS] PoC successful. The claim succeeded on-chain, but the funds are permanently locked in the recipient contract due to the silent failure.\n\nSuite result: ok. 1 passed; 0 failed; 0 skipped; finished in 14.38ms (4.35ms CPU time)\n```",
